# Prod Odoo tickets over the Odoo connector

- All prod task operations go through the **Odoo connector** — the claude.ai
  connector to production, signed in as the developer (their own account and
  rights) — never claude-in-chrome. Its tools are `search_read`, `read`,
  `read_group`, `create`, `write`, `message_post`, `begin_upload`, `execute`,
  `get_connection_status`; there is no connect step and no profile to restore.
  If it is missing or asks to authenticate, tell the user to (re)connect it in
  their Claude settings — never ask for an Odoo password or API key.
- Identity (login, uid, default project, stages, chat) comes from memory node
  `user_identity` — read it first, never hardcode a person. `get_connection_status`
  says who the connector is signed in as.

## Connection & context flags

- **Every create/write/note goes through the plain tools — `create`, `write`,
  `message_post` — with the quiet context**, written `<quiet>` below:
  `{"mail_create_nolog": true, "mail_create_nosubscribe": true,
  "mail_auto_subscribe_no_notify": true}` — add `"tracking_disable": true`
{% if task_ai_driven %}
  on stage moves and other tracked fields. Why: the assign-notify guard from
  PR 770 keys off `mail_auto_subscribe_no_notify`, and a ticket write is
  plumbing, not news.
{% else %}
  on stage moves and other tracked fields. Why: `mail_auto_subscribe_no_notify`
  stops Odoo's "assigned to you" mail, and a ticket write is plumbing, not news.
{% endif %}
- **Not through `execute`.** It runs any method on any model, so it is
  confirmed on every call (an organisation can make that mandatory), and a
  plain write sent through it stops on a prompt. The plain tools take a
  `context` limited to the chatter-quieting keys (`mail_create_nolog`,
  `mail_create_nosubscribe`, `mail_auto_subscribe_no_notify`, `mail_notrack`,
  `tracking_disable`, `lang`, `tz`) and refuse any other; `execute` stays for
  real method calls and for a context key outside that set.
- **Fallback — no `context` on `write`:** a session opened before the
  connector's tool list refreshed (or an older connector) shows `write` and
  `create` without a `context` parameter and has no `message_post`. Then, and
  only then, send the same call through `execute`:
  `write(m, ids, vals, context=c)` → `execute(m, "write", [ids, vals], {"context": c})`;
  `create(m, vals, context=c)` → `execute(m, "create", [vals], {"context": c})`
  (answers the bare id); `message_post(m, id, body, attachment_ids=a, context=c)` →
  `execute(m, "message_post", [[id]], {"body": body, "message_type": "comment",
  "subtype_xmlid": "mail.mt_note", "attachment_ids": a, "context": c})`.
- **Cost rule:** never include `description` / {% if task_ai_driven %}`solution` / {% endif %}any Html field in
  a multi-record `search_read` — prod descriptions embed base64 images
  (megabytes). Read heavy fields for ONE record at a time, explicit lists only.

## Resolve a ticket

{% if task_keys %}
{{ ticket_prefix }} key → id: `search_read` `project.task` `[["key", "=", "{{ ticket_prefix }}-xxxx"]]`
fields `["id", "name", "stage_id", "user_ids"]`. From a URL, take `id=<n>`.
{% else %}
This Odoo gives tasks no key (neither project_ai_driven 16.0.2.17.0 or later nor
OCA project_key), so `project.task` has no `key` field: never put `key` in a
domain or a field list. A task's reference is `{{ ticket_prefix }}-<id>`, its
record id behind the prefix; wherever a skill says key, it means this
reference. Reference → record: drop the prefix, then `read [<id>]` fields
`["id", "name", "stage_id", "user_ids"]`. From a URL, take `id=<n>`.
{% endif %}

{% if task_ai_driven %}
## Read a task: the brief

`project.task` `brief` (`project_ai_driven` 16.0.2.14.0 or later) is the whole task as Markdown in
one read, computed for you: `read` `project.task` `[<id>]` `["brief"]`. In order: the header ({% if task_keys %}key{% else %}`#<id>`{% endif %},
title, project, stage and since when, type, estimate{% if ai_estimate %}, size and Estimate Coefficient{% else %} and size{% endif %}, priority, planned window, deadline,
{% if ai_estimate %}Actual Hours, timesheet hours{% else %}hours spent{% endif %}, assignees, UAT users where the field exists, author, Resolution, modules on a task of
the project that develops this Odoo, tags, the form link), related tasks (parent,
sub-tasks, blocked by, blocking), pull requests, description, question rows, solution, checklist with
its progress, test cases with their results, sessions, the last 10 internal notes and the
attachments. Question rows, checklist items, test cases and attachments carry their ids, so the
writes that follow need no second read.

- An attachment shows as a pointer: `![image: <name>](attachment <id>)`, `[file: <name>](attachment <id>)`
  (without the name where it cannot be read, or with the link's text): read the file from
  `ir.attachment` by that id when you need it. An image still inline in an old text shows as
  `[image not yet moved to an attachment]`.
- Urls of links and images lose their access tokens, urls in text on a best-effort basis (the
  field's help says which may keep one): paste a brief only where the task's readers may read it.
- Name the field: a `read` or `search_read` without a field list leaves it out, and it is not
  exportable. One task per read: the task you work on, not a list.
- A database without the field (`project_ai_driven` older than 16.0.2.14.0, or a branch it is not
  ported to yet) reads the parts one by one: `read` `["name", {% if task_keys %}"key", {% endif %}"stage_id", "user_ids",
  "task_type", "tshirt_estimate", "{{ hours }}", "effective_hours", "date_deadline", "child_ids",
  "depend_on_ids"]`, then `["description"]` and `["solution"]` (heavy Html: one task at a time); the
  question rows as the Technical Solution tab section reads them; test cases: `search_read`
  `project.task.testcase` `[["task_id", "=", <id>]]` fields `["name", "state", "verification_kind",
  "test_reference", "steps_to_execute", "expected_results"]`; checklist: `search_read`
  `project.task.checklist.item` `[["task_id", "=", <id>]]` fields `["name", "state", "user_id"]`.

{% endif %}## Creating a task — field conventions

| Field | Convention |
|---|---|
| `name` | Imperative summary of the work. No {{ ticket_prefix }} prefix — {% if task_keys %}the task key adds it{% else %}the reference `{{ ticket_prefix }}-<id>` carries it{% endif %}. |
| `project_id` | Default project from `user_identity`. |
| `stage_id` | Backlog for queued work; Development only if work starts immediately. Resolve the ids by name (`project.task.type`). |
| `user_ids` | `[[6, 0, [<uid>]]]` — **always the human owner**: you (uid from `user_identity`), or the teammate the user hands the task to. Never empty on purpose. An owner who is not you gets a [handover note](#handover-note-assignee-is-not-you). |
{% if task_ai_driven %}
| `task_type` | **Always set on create**: the field has no default. Honest pick: `bug` (broken behavior) / `feature` (net-new) / `improvement` (enhance existing) / `investigation` / `technical_debt` / `support` / `milestone`. Required before the task enters a stage of the type and estimate rule (under this table). |
{% endif %}
| `tag_ids` | **Always set.** `[[6, 0, [ids]]]` on create. ≥1 **area** tag + a **feature/topic** tag for any multi-ticket effort so siblings group. Reuse existing tags — never a near-duplicate. See [Tags](#tags-projecttags). |
{% if task_ai_driven %}
| `{{ hours }}` | The estimate, in hours: take it from the **`estimate` skill** and write it here. Above zero before the task enters a stage of the type and estimate rule (under this table). `tshirt_estimate` (Size) is never written: Odoo computes it from these hours (≤2h `xs`, ≤6h `s`, ≤12h `m`, ≤32h `l`, ≤60h `xl`, above that `xxl`; no hours → empty) and ignores a value sent for it. |
{% if ai_estimate %}
| `estimate_coefficient` | Written with `{{ hours }}`, in the same write, by the `estimate` skill: the written hours / the anchor price they came from. A write that changes `{{ hours }}` without it empties it (the same value keeps it): an estimate not priced from the anchors (a number someone gives, a hand edit of the Estimate) has none, and the coefficient's median skips the task. A re-estimate writes both again. Not on the form. |
| `actual_hours` | Never on create. The focused hours the task took to done, waits excluded (the `estimate` skill's Calibration loop), written in the closing write. Above zero before the task enters a stage of the Actual Hours rule (under this table); never negative. |
{% endif %}
| `priority_level` | `"4"` Highest … `"0"` Lowest; default `"1"`. |
{% else %}
| `{{ hours }}` | The estimate in hours (`estimate` skill). |
{% endif %}
{% if task_timeline %}
| **Planned Dates** — `planned_date_start` / `planned_date_end` (both `datetime`) | The scheduled work window: drives the Gantt, team dashboard and overdue colouring, and `planned_date_end` is the **deadline** the dashboard shows. Set both for any task in progress / with a planned slot (`"YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS"`, in UTC: pick the hour in the user's time zone; day-level granularity is enough). |
| `date_deadline` (`{{ deadline_type }}`) | {% if team_dashboard_deadline %}In a project flagged AI-driven it **follows `planned_date_end` on its own** ({% if odoo_version == '16.0' %}the date in the saving user's time zone{% else %}{{ deadline_of_end }}{% endif %}) whenever its planned end or its project changes, a rolled-up parent's included: write the Planned Dates, not the Deadline; a Deadline written by hand stays until then, and clearing the planned end keeps it. Elsewhere keep it **in sync with `planned_date_end`** ({{ deadline_of_end }});{% else %}Keep **in sync with `planned_date_end`** ({{ deadline_of_end }});{% endif %} the status ETA reads it. Don't set it alone — set the Planned Dates window too, or the Gantt/dashboard stay empty. |
| `is_planned_from_subtasks` (read only, {% if use_team_dashboard %}from `project_team_dashboard`, which rolls planned dates up in every project{% else %}where a module rolls planned dates up; check with `fields_get` before reading it{% endif %}) | True on a task with active sub-tasks: its Planned Dates are the span of theirs (earliest start, latest end) and stay **empty while none of them is dated**. Odoo refuses a write that changes them: date the sub-tasks instead. {% if team_dashboard_deadline %}In a project flagged AI-driven its `date_deadline` follows the rolled-up `planned_date_end` on its own; elsewhere it is not rolled up: keep it in sync with the rolled-up end ({{ deadline_of_end }}).{% else %}`date_deadline` is not rolled up: keep it in sync with the rolled-up `planned_date_end` ({{ deadline_of_end }}).{% endif %} |
{% else %}
| `date_deadline` (`{{ deadline_type }}`) | The deadline; the status ETA reads it. |
{% endif %}
| `description` | **Full copy** of the source content (for memory mirrors: state line verbatim + the journal excerpt / node body), simple HTML (`<p>`, `<code>`), footer `Claude memory item [ID] — see journal/<date>`. |
{% if task_ai_driven %}
| `resolution` | Never on create: empty until the task closes. `code` (Code) / `data` (Data) / `configuration` (Configuration) / `no_change` (No change). See [Pull requests and resolution](#pull-requests-projectpullrequest-and-resolution). |
| `pr_uri` | Never on create. Reads the link of the task's first pull request. A write takes one pull request link and adds that pull request to the task. Never text. |
| `pull_request_ids` | The task's pull requests (many2many to `project.pull.request`). Read it; link a pull request through `pr_uri`. |
| `pr_legacy` | Read-only: the text `pr_uri` held before pull requests were records. |
| `module_ids` | Never on create. The installed modules the task touches, filled from its pull requests, on a task of the project that develops this Odoo only. See [Modules of a task](#modules-of-a-task-module_ids). |
| `session_ids` | Never on create. The agent sessions that worked on the task, a row each. See [Sessions of a task](#sessions-of-a-task-session_ids). |

**The type and estimate rule.** In a project flagged AI-driven, a task enters a stage the
project lists under "Stages Requiring a Type and an Estimate" only with a `task_type` and
`{{ hours }}` above zero. It is checked on a create into such a stage, on a change of
stage and on a change of project that moves the stage; a write that fails it is refused.
A task in a stage outside that list may have neither.
{% if ai_estimate %}

**The Actual Hours rule.** In a project flagged AI-driven, a task enters a stage the
project lists under "Stages Requiring Actual Hours" only with `actual_hours` above zero:
timesheet hours do not count. Checked at the same moments as the type and estimate rule;
a task already in the stage keeps every other edit, and clearing its `actual_hours` there
is allowed.
{% endif %}

Tabs on the form map to: Description → `description`, Technical Solution →
question rows `project.task.question` (`question_ids`) above `solution` (Html),
Test Cases → `project.task.testcase`, Checklist → `project.task.checklist.item`
(`checklist_item_ids`).

## Technical Solution tab — clarifying questions and `solution`

The tab shows the task's clarifying questions, one `project.task.question` row
each (task field `question_ids`), above `solution`: the technical solution /
implementation plan, an Html field written on the task.

| Field on `project.task.question` | Holds |
|---|---|
| `task_id` | The task (required). |
| `round` | The round it was asked in, a name or a date: `"Alignment round, 2026-11-12"`. |
| `question` | The question (Html). |
| `addressed_to_id` | Who is asked (`res.users`); empty when nobody in particular. |
| `answer` | The answer (Html). |
| `answered_by_id`, `answered_on` | Who answered and when, stamped by the write that answers the row. |
| `state` | `open` (default) / `answered` / `dropped`. |
| `sequence` | The order on the tab. |

**Always persist clarifying Q&A.** Whenever you ask the user clarifying
questions while creating or refining a ticket — standalone or inside the
`pipeline` — each question is a row and each answer goes on its row. Don't let
a clarification live only in the chat. Every call with `<quiet>`:

- **Ask a round**: one `create` per question, the same `round` on each:
  ```
  create(model="project.task.question", values={"task_id": <id>,
    "round": "Alignment round, 2026-11-12",
    "question": "<p>Which warehouses are in scope?</p>",
    "addressed_to_id": <asked uid>}, context=<quiet>)
  ```
- **Record the answer**: `write` `project.task.question` `[<row id>]`
  `{"answer": "<p>Only the main warehouse.</p>"}`. The row turns `answered`
  and stamps `answered_by_id` / `answered_on` with you and now; an answer
  someone else gave carries their `answered_by_id` in the same write.
- **Drop a question** that no longer applies: `write {"state": "dropped"}`.
  Back to `open` clears who answered and when.
- **Read them**: `search_read` `project.task.question` `[["task_id", "=", <id>]]`
  fields `["round", "question", "addressed_to_id", "answer", "answered_by_id",
  "answered_on", "state"]`, order `"sequence, id"`.

A round asked before the task existed: create its rows right after the task.
Earlier rounds are never rewritten: a follow-up, or an answer that changes, is
a row of a new round. When an answer changes scope or a design choice, also
post the matching internal note (`Requirement Change` / `Design Decision`).
{% else %}

## Clarifying Q&A, technical solution, test cases — internal notes

`project.task` on this Odoo has no fields for them: each is an internal note
(`message_post`, see [Internal notes](#internal-notes-chatter)) with its bold
category, one note per round, so earlier rounds stay as they were.

| Category | Holds |
|---|---|
| `Design Decision` | A round of clarifying questions and the answers received, as parallel numbered lists (Q1↔A1). |
| `Technical Solution` | The technical solution / implementation plan. |
| `Test Cases` | The test cases, numbered: name, steps to execute, expected result. |
| `Test Case Verification` | `X/Y passed`, then per case: passed / failed (what failed) / not applicable, and what was checked. |

**Always persist clarifying Q&A.** Whenever you ask the user clarifying
questions while creating or refining a ticket — standalone or inside the
`pipeline` — post BOTH sides as one `Design Decision` note. Don't let a
clarification live only in the chat:

```
message_post(model="project.task", res_id=<id>,
  body="<p><b>Design Decision</b> — clarifying Q&amp;A</p>"
       "<p>Questions:</p><ol><li>Which warehouses are in scope?</li><li>Block or only warn on conflict?</li></ol>"
       "<p>Answers:</p><ol><li>Only the main warehouse.</li><li>Hard block.</li></ol>",
  context={"mail_create_nosubscribe": true})
```

When an answer changes scope, also post a `Requirement Change` note. A
clarifying exchange that happened before the task existed goes into a note
right after `create`.

**Read them back:** `search_read` `mail.message`
`[["model", "=", "project.task"], ["res_id", "=", <id>], ["message_type", "=", "comment"]]`
fields `["date", "author_id", "body"]`, order `"date asc"`.
{% endif %}

## Tags (`project.tags`)

Set on **every** task you create; back-fill when you touch an untagged sibling.
`project.tags` is **global** (shared across all projects) — reuse, never duplicate.

**Two layers, both written through `tag_ids`:**

- **Area** (≥1, required) — reuse the coarse vocabulary, never a near-duplicate.
{% if task_ai_driven %}
  Current vocab: `Accounting`, `SCM`, `Inventory`, `Manufacturing`, `Purchases`,
  `Sales`, `Quality`, `Project Management`, `Human Resource`, `Engineering`,
  `Website`, `Business Operations`, `Product Lifecycle Management`, plus type-ish
  `Bug` / `Feature` / `Custom Report` / `Customization` / `Support` / `NeedAssistance`.
{% else %}
  The vocabulary is the existing `project.tags` names (`search_read` them first).
{% endif %}
- **Feature / topic** (set on any multi-ticket effort) — a specific tag clustering
  one body of work, e.g. `Cash Flow Forecast`. Create **once**, reuse the exact
  same tag on every related ticket — turns "show me every cashflow ticket" into a
  one-click filter.

**Reuse discipline** (avoid `Cashflow` vs `Cash Flow` vs `Cash Flow Forecast`):
search before creating, match case-insensitively, reuse exact hits, create only when
genuinely new. Tags are an index, not free-text labels.

**Resolve-or-create a tag:**

```
hit = search_read("project.tags", [["name", "=ilike", "Cash Flow Forecast"]], ["id", "name"])
tag_id = hit[0].id  if hit  else  create(model="project.tags", values={"name": "Cash Flow Forecast", "color": 4})["id"]
```

**Write tags** — on create pass the whole set, `"tag_ids": [[6, 0, [<id1>, <id2>]]]`.
To add a tag to an existing ticket non-destructively (keeps current tags), use
`(4, id)` with the quiet context:

```
write(model="project.task", ids=[<task_id>], values={"tag_ids": [[4, <tag_id>]]},
      context=<quiet> + {"tracking_disable": true})
```

## Internal notes (chatter)

Posted **as the developer** with the connector's `message_post`: it logs what
the chatter's *Log note* button posts, and its author is always whoever the
connector is signed in as. It takes no `author_id`, `email_from` or mentions,
so a note cannot come out under another name. `mail_create_nosubscribe` keeps
you off the task's followers:

```
message_post(model="project.task", res_id=<task_id>,
             body="<p><b>Category</b> — text…</p>",
             context={"mail_create_nosubscribe": true})
```

{% if task_ai_driven %}
Bold **Category** prefix: `Requirement Change` / `Design Decision` /
`Bug Fix` / `Review Fix` / `User Feedback` / `Progress` / `Revised` (title and
description revised before Delivered — see below) / `Deploy` (the exact
commands a deploy ran — see the `deploy` skill). The bold first words are the
note's category: an internal note of a task opening with one of them carries it
in `mail.message` `note_category` (`requirement_change`, `design_decision`,
`bug_fix`, `review_fix`, `user_feedback`, `progress`, `revised`, `deploy`); any
other opening has none. A task's notes of one category:
`search_read` `mail.message` `[["model", "=", "project.task"], ["res_id", "=", <id>],
["note_category", "=", "design_decision"]]` fields `["date", "author_id", "body"]`
(project_ai_driven 16.0.2.8.0 or later). In Odoo: Project → Task Notes, or the
task's Notes button.
{% else %}
Bold **Category** prefix: `Requirement Change` / `Design Decision` /
`Bug Fix` / `Review Fix` / `User Feedback` / `Progress` / `Revised` (title and
description revised before Delivered — see below) / `Technical Solution` /
`Test Cases` / `Test Case Verification` / `Deploy` (the exact commands a deploy
ran — see the `deploy` skill).
{% endif %}

## Handover note (assignee is not you)

A task created for someone else, or reassigned to someone else, makes them a
follower and sends them nothing: `mail_auto_subscribe_no_notify` in `<quiet>`
suppresses Odoo's "assigned to you" mail, and the chatter stays empty. Right
after that `create` or `user_ids` write, post one note per task that names them:

1. Partner: `read` `res.users` `[<assignee uid>]` `["partner_id"]`. Several new
   assignees → one note, all their partners.
2. Draft the body in English, as the user, opening with the assignee's first
   name: the task is theirs now, where it came from, why them, whether you
   still track it. No em dashes, no contractions, no AI phrasing. Show the
   draft to the user; post on their go.
3. Post through `execute` (the connector's `message_post` mentions nobody).
   It returns the message id. The note mails the partners in `partner_ids`;
   other followers get it only if they follow notes.

   ```
   execute(model="project.task", method="message_post", args=[[<task_id>]],
           kwargs={"body": "<p>…</p>", "message_type": "comment",
                   "subtype_xmlid": "mail.mt_note", "partner_ids": [<partner_id>],
                   "context": {"mail_create_nosubscribe": true}})
   ```

4. Read back: `search_read` `mail.notification`
   `[["mail_message_id", "=", <message id>]]` fields `["res_partner_id",
   "notification_type", "notification_status", "failure_type"]`. Expect one
   `email` row per partner, status `sent` (`ready` = still queued, read again).
   `exception` / `bounce` → tell the user.

{% if task_ai_driven %}
## Checklist (`project.task.checklist.item`)

- Create: `{"task_id": <id>, "name": "<step>", "user_id": <owner uid>}`;
  without `user_id` the item is yours; optional `deadline`. The task lists its
  items in `checklist_item_ids`.
- States: `todo` (default) / `done` / `waiting` / `cancelled` — complete a
  step with `write {"state": "done"}`.
- Only the item's assignee or reviewer, as they were before the write,
  changes its `state`, `name` or `user_id`. An item is found and read by
  whoever can read its task, in the companies selected for the call.
- Both with `<quiet>`: without `mail_auto_subscribe_no_notify`, a create and
  every change of `state`, `name` or `user_id` notify the item's assignee and
  reviewer, and on a reassignment the former assignee, never you.

## Test cases (`project.task.testcase`)

- Create: `{"task_id": <id>, "name": "<short scenario>",
  "steps_to_execute": "<ol>…</ol>", "expected_results": "<p>…</p>"}` —
  state defaults to `not_run`.
- The result is the `state`: `not_run` / `passed` / `failed` / `waiting` /
  `not_applicable`. With it: `verified_by_id`, `verified_on`,
  `verification_kind` (`manual` / `automated` / `demo`), `test_reference`.
  The result has no passed flag, and `todo` / `done` / `cancelled` are refused
  (`project_ai_driven` 16.0.2.15.0 or later; a branch not ported yet still has both).
- Record a result: `write {"state": "passed", "verification_kind": "<kind>"}`;
  `failed` the same way, what failed in `comments`; a case that does not apply →
  `state: "not_applicable"`. A write of `passed` or `failed` stamps
  `verified_by_id` and `verified_on` with you and now, also on a case already in
  that state.
  - `<kind>`: `automated` when a test proves the case, with `"test_reference":
    "<selector>"` in the same write (e.g. `/module:TestClass.test_method`);
    `manual` for a check by hand (shell, UI); `demo` for one the demo video
    shows. A reference written earlier stays until a write replaces it.
- Back to `not_run` clears verified by, on and the kind; the reference stays.

## Pull requests (`project.pull.request`) and resolution

A pull request is a record of its own, linked to every task it belongs to.

| Field on `project.pull.request` | Holds |
|---|---|
| `url` | The link, stored canonical, unique. |
| `repository`, `number` | Read from `url`. |
| `state` | `open` / `merged` / `closed`. |
| `merge_date`, `deploy_date` | Datetime, UTC, `"YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS"`. |
| `task_ids` | The tasks it belongs to (many2many; the task's side is `pull_request_ids`). |

Project users may read, create and write these records, not delete them.

- **Link a pull request to a task** right after it is opened, in any repository:
  `write` `project.task` `[<task_id>]` `{"pr_uri": "<PR URL>"}` with `<quiet>`. The write
  finds the record of that link, or creates it in state `open`, and links it to the task.
  It adds, it does not replace: a second link written to the same task is a second pull
  request on it. One link per write, one write per task the pull request belongs to.
- **Accepted links**: `.../pull/N`, `.../merge_requests/N`, `.../pull-requests/N`,
  `.../pulls/N`. Any other value is refused with a ValidationError: `memory://...`, `N/A`,
  plain text, a link that is not a pull request, two links in one write.
- **Read** a task's pull requests: `read` `project.task` `["pull_request_ids"]`, then
  `read` `project.pull.request` `["url", "state", "merge_date", "deploy_date"]`. `pr_uri`
  reads the first one's link only; `pr_legacy` is read-only old text.
- **State and dates** follow the pull request: `merged` and `merge_date` once it is merged,
  `deploy_date` once it is deployed.{% if prod_ssh_host and custom_repo_name %} The `deploy` skill writes them (Step 8).{% endif +%}

**The closing rule.** In a project flagged AI-driven, a task enters a stage the project
lists under "Stages Requiring a Resolution" only with a `resolution`:

| `resolution` | Label | Means | Pull request |
|---|---|---|---|
| `code` | Code | The task was resolved by a code change. | At least one on the task. A task with a pull request and no `resolution` gets `code` from the rule. |
| `data` | Data | The fix was a data change: a script or records edited. | Not needed. |
| `configuration` | Configuration | Settings changed, no code. | Not needed. |
| `no_change` | No change | Nothing changed: answered, duplicate, not reproducible, declined. | Not needed. |

A task with no pull request and no `resolution` does not enter such a stage: the write
that moves it carries `resolution` (see [Lifecycle](#lifecycle--stage-moves)).

## Modules of a task (`module_ids`)

The installed modules a task touches (`project_ai_driven` 16.0.2.10.0 or later): `module_ids`,
many2many to `ir.module.module`, read and written by project users. `module_names` gives their
technical names in one read, sorted and joined with ", " (`"project_ai_driven, sale_stock"`),
`false` on a task without modules.

They belong to the one AI-driven project that develops this Odoo database: the project flag
`develops_this_odoo` (Develops This Odoo), at most one project per database, set by a Settings
administrator. The task reads it too (`read` `project.task` `[<id>]` `["develops_this_odoo"]`):
`false` → leave its Modules alone, the form hides them there.

- **Fill from a pull request**, on a task whose `develops_this_odoo` is true: the top-level
  directory of each file the pull request changes is a candidate name:
  `gh api repos/<owner>/<repo>/pulls/<n>/files --paginate --jq '.[].filename | split("/")[0]' | sort -u`.
  `search_read` `ir.module.module`
  `[["name", "in", [<names>]], ["state", "in", ["installed", "to upgrade", "to remove"]]]`
  fields `["id", "name"]`, then `write` `project.task` `[<task_id>]`
  `{"module_ids": [[4, <id>], ...]}` with `<quiet>`, one `[4, id]` per module found. `[4, id]`
  only adds: what is on the task stays. A name that is no installed module (`docs`, a file at
  the root, a module not installed yet) is left out.
- **Correct by hand**: the Modules tags on the task form. Adding a module that is not installed
  is refused; a module uninstalled after it was added stays on the task. A module a person
  removed comes back when a later fill names it again.
- **Read**: `read` `project.task` `[<id>]` `["module_names"]`.

## Sessions of a task (`session_ids`)

The agent sessions that worked on a task, one row each (`project_ai_driven` 16.0.2.12.0 or later):
`project.task.session`, read and written by whoever can read its task; the Sessions tab of the form
on an AI-driven project.

| Field | Holds |
|---|---|
| `task_id` | The task (required). |
| `session_id` | The agent session id (required): `echo $CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID`; where `entire` records sessions, `entire session info <id>` opens it. No link is stored. |
| `date` | When the row was written last (default now). |
| `user_id` | Who worked (default the writer). |
| `outcome` | One line: what the session did for the task. |

- **Write** when a session closes (`pipeline` Phase 7 Step 0b, a child before its report):
  `create` `project.task.session` `{"task_id": <id>, "session_id": "<id>", "outcome": "<line>"}`
  with `<quiet>`. One row per task and session: a `create` naming a task and session that already
  have a row updates that row with the values given (`date` now unless given) and returns it. Editing a
  row by hand (`write`) changes only what it names.
- **Read**: `search_read` `project.task.session` `[["task_id", "=", <id>]]`
  fields `["date", "user_id", "session_id", "outcome"]`.
{% else %}
## Test cases (internal notes)

- Write: one `Test Cases` note, the cases numbered, each its name, steps to
  execute and expected result.
- Verify: one `Test Case Verification` note per verification run.
