## Attachments (screenshots, demo videos, executed scripts, exports)

Generic mechanism — any local file. **Use `begin_upload` when the connector
offers it** (odoo-fast-mcp with sign-in): the file goes from the shell straight
to the server, never through the conversation, and lands byte for byte.

```
link = begin_upload(model="project.task", res_id=<id>, filename="<file>")
# Bash: run link["command"] with FILE replaced by the path, i.e.
#   curl -sS --fail-with-body --data-binary @<path> '<upload_url>'
# -> {"id": <att>, "checksum": "<sha1>", "file_size": ..., ...}
message_post(model="project.task", res_id=<id>, body="<p>…</p>",
             attachment_ids=[<att>, ...], context={"mail_create_nosubscribe": true})
```

- One link per file: single use, 10 minutes, 20 MB. Several files: a link
  each, then one note carrying all their ids.
- Check `checksum` against `sha1sum <path>`; a mismatch means the file did not
  arrive intact, so upload it again. Images are stored as sent, not shrunk.
- `mimetype` is optional; Odoo infers it from the name.
- A 401 means the connector session has no stored credential (reconnect it);
  a 403 means your account may not change that record.

**Without `begin_upload`** (another Odoo MCP, or no shell): base64 through
`create`, for **small files only** (a few hundred KB). The bytes pass through
the model's context, cost tokens and can be corrupted on the way. Pick
`mimetype` per kind: `image/png`, `image/gif`, `text/x-python` (scripts),
`text/plain`, `text/csv`.

```
b64 = base64 of the local file
att = create(model="ir.attachment", values={"name": "<file>",
      "datas": b64, "res_model": "project.task", "res_id": <id>,
      "mimetype": "<mimetype>"})["id"]
message_post(model="project.task", res_id=<id>, body="<p>…</p>",
             attachment_ids=[att], context={"mail_create_nosubscribe": true})
```

Then read the attachment's `checksum` and compare it with `sha1sum`. A file
too big for this path stays off the ticket: say so and where it is, never post
a truncated or re-typed copy.

### Always attach scripts you run against prod

Ran a script as part of a task? Attach it. Covers any **Odoo shell**
(`{{ odoo_cmd }} shell` / `odoo-bin shell`, local or over SSH on prod) and any
**multi-record data-mutating RPC** (bulk `write`/`unlink`, `_merge`, mass state
change). No silent prod mutation: if it changed data and isn't one trivial
write, the script goes on the task as a record of what was run.

- **Save the exact code** that ran, verbatim — including dry-run guard, batch
  size, savepoint/`commit`. File `<{{ ticket_prefix }}-key>_<what>.py`
  (e.g. `{{ ticket_prefix }}-1117_partner_merge.py`), mimetype `text/x-python`.
- **Attach when you run it**, not only at close: attach the script + post a
  `Progress` / `Bug Fix` note stating model, records affected, dry-run vs
  committed, result counts. Note + file together are the audit trail.
- Re-ran a changed version? Attach the final one that actually ran; if behaviour
  differed materially between runs, attach each distinct version.

## Revise title and description before Delivered

A ticket is filed from what was known at the time: the reporter's first
guess, or a ticket written from that guess. The work finds out what the
problem really was, and often it was something else. A Delivered ticket is
what the team searches and counts later, so check it reads true before the
Delivered write (revising earlier, once the real problem is clear, is fine):

1. Read it: `read` `project.task` `[<id>]` `["name"{% if task_ai_driven %}, "task_type"{% endif %}]`, and
   `search_count` `[["id", "=", <id>], ["description", "ilike", "data:image"]]`.
   `0` → `read` `["description"]` too. `1` → the description carries pasted
   screenshots as base64 (megabytes, see the cost rule): leave it unread and
   as filed.
2. Hold it against what the work established: the root cause, what changed,
   the PR. It needs revising when the title names the wrong cause, document,
   model or module, or when the scope narrowed, widened or was split off.
{% if task_ai_driven %}
   So does a reported `bug` that turned out to be data, setup or user error:
   bug counts are taken from `task_type`.
{% endif %}
   Still true → say so in one line and close without a write.
3. Draft:
   - `name` — the imperative summary of what was actually done, as on create.
   - `description` — what the problem turned out to be and what changed, in
     simple HTML, then the original body unchanged under `<h3>As filed</h3>`,
     so the reporter's words stay searchable. An image-bearing description is
     not rewritten; its revised account goes into the note (step 5).
{% if task_ai_driven %}
   - `task_type` — only if it changed.
4. Show the user the old → new title, the new top of the description and any
   `task_type` change; write on their go, with `<quiet>` + `tracking_disable`.
{% else %}
4. Show the user the old → new title and the new top of the description;
   write on their go, with `<quiet>` + `tracking_disable`.
{% endif %}
5. Post a `Revised` note: the old title, and in a sentence or two what the
   ticket turned out to be (for an image-bearing description, the full revised
   account). `tracking_disable` keeps the title change out of the chatter, so
   this note is the only place the old title survives.

## Lifecycle / stage moves

- **Start of work** (the user says to start / take into work a ticket or
  a memory item with `[odoo:ID]`):
  1. `write {"stage_id": <Development>}` (`<quiet>` + `tracking_disable`);
{% if task_ai_driven %}
     before it the task has a `task_type` and `{{ hours }}` above zero (`read` both),
     or this same write sets the missing one (`task_type`: the honest pick,
     `{{ hours }}`: the `estimate` skill{% if ai_estimate %}, with its `estimate_coefficient`{% endif %}). A stage under
     [the type and estimate rule](#creating-a-task--field-conventions) refuses the
     move without both;
{% endif %}
  2. assign the owner if not yet assigned (`user_ids`, `<quiet>`);
{% if task_timeline %}
  3. **set the Planned Dates window** so the ticket lands on the schedule:
     `planned_date_start` = now, `planned_date_end` = now + the estimate
     (`{{ hours }}` at ~6 focused h/day). A ticket taken into
     work with no Planned Dates is invisible on the Gantt/dashboard.
     A task whose `is_planned_from_subtasks` is true gets no window of its own
     (the write is refused): its open sub-tasks get theirs (see
     [Sub-tasks & blockers](#sub-tasks--blockers-tasktask-links)).
{% if team_dashboard_deadline %}
     In a project flagged AI-driven `date_deadline` follows `planned_date_end`
     on its own (a rolled-up parent's, the latest end of its sub-tasks): leave
     it out of the write. Elsewhere write it by hand, in sync with that end
     ({{ deadline_of_end }}).
{% else %}
     Write `date_deadline` by hand, in sync with `planned_date_end`
     ({{ deadline_of_end }}); on a rolled-up parent, with the latest end of its
     sub-tasks.
{% endif %}
  4. **rebalance the user's other deadlines** — the new in-scope work pushes
     existing tickets out: run `my-status` `rebalance` (shifts their Planned
     Dates windows). See `feedback_rebalance_deadlines_on_scope_growth`.
{% else %}
  3. **set the deadline**: `date_deadline` = today + the estimate
     (`{{ hours }}` at ~6 focused h/day).
  4. **rebalance the user's other deadlines** — the new in-scope work pushes
     existing tickets out: run `my-status` `rebalance` (shifts their
     `date_deadline`). See `feedback_rebalance_deadlines_on_scope_growth`.
{% endif %}
  5. **send the Teams status** — run the `my-status` skill `send` flow (current
     ticket + ETA + next up, draft → explicit go → IT team chat). In the
     `pipeline` this is a **hard gate**: if Teams isn't authenticated, pause,
     run the re-auth yourself (never hand the command to the user), send, then
     proceed.
{% if use_kb %}
  6. **load what kb knows** about the ticket's areas. Its kb record is the one `kb sync odoo`
     made, with the {% if task_keys %}key{% else %}record id{% endif %} as `external_id`:
     `kb query 'MATCH (r:Record {external_id: $k}) RETURN r.id AS id' -p k=<{% if task_keys %}KEY{% else %}record id{% endif %}>`. Then
     `kb context <id>`: the record's area tags, the knowledge records that share them
     (`[id] kind title — summary`; `kb show <id>` reads one in full) and the other open
     tasks there. {% if task_ai_driven %}The sync tags a ticket with what Odoo already says: an area for each module of
     the task's Modules named like an area of the vocabulary, and the type `bug` or `feature`
     from its task type{% else %}The sync tags a ticket with the type `bug` or `feature` from its task type{% endif %}. `kb sync odoo --dry-run` prints what a sync would change,
     record by record, and writes nothing.
     - **Exit 4: the record has no area tag yet**{% if task_ai_driven %}: its task has no Modules, or none named like an
       area{% endif %}. Choose the area from the ticket: the module or subsystem its title and
       description are about, from the vocabulary `kb tags` lists (the project's
       `.kb/tags.toml` plus kb's built-in areas). `kb edit <id> --tag <area>`, then
       `kb context <id>` again. When no area fits, ask the user: a new area is a line in
       `.kb/tags.toml`, added through a PR.
     - A task that an open task blocks is silent: `kb today` and `kb next` leave it out,
       `kb next --all` lists it, and `kb why <id>` names the blocker.
     - A kb without the `context` command: say so in one line and go on.
{% endif %}
  Stage-move + {% if task_timeline %}Planned Dates{% else %}deadline{% endif %} + rebalance + sent status are a mandatory set;
  don't do one without the others.
{% if task_ai_driven %}
- **Close as done** → [revise the title and description](#revise-title-and-description-before-delivered)
  to what the work found, then the closing write (`<quiet>` + `tracking_disable`).
  Before closing a task that ran prod shell / bulk-data scripts, confirm each is
  attached (see Attachments).
{% if ai_estimate %}
  - **Actual Hours go in the closing write**, in every case below: the focused hours
    the `estimate` skill's Calibration loop counts. A stage under
    [the Actual Hours rule](#creating-a-task--field-conventions) refuses the move
    without them.
{% endif %}
  - **With a pull request** → `{"stage_id": <Delivered>{{ actual }}}`. Its link went into `pr_uri`
    when the pull request was opened; a pull request missing from the task's
    `pull_request_ids` gets its link written first. The closing rule sets `resolution`
    to `code` there. A task whose pull request carries only the script of a data fix
    closes with `{"stage_id": <Delivered>, "resolution": "data"{{ actual }}}`: the pull request stays
    on the task, and the resolution says what resolved it.
  - **A pull request of the task still `open`** → before the closing write, bring its
    record in line with the pull request (`gh pr view <url> --json state,mergedAt`), with
    `<quiet>`: merged → `{"state": "merged", "merge_date": "<mergedAt in UTC, YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS>"}`;
    closed without a merge → `{"state": "closed"}`; still open → leave it and say so.
{% if prod_ssh_host and custom_repo_name %}
    The `deploy` skill has stamped the ones it landed; this is for a pull request merged
    outside a deploy, in this project's own repo or another one.
{% endif %}
  - **Without a pull request** → `{"stage_id": <Delivered>, "resolution": "<key>"{{ actual }}}`, one
    write. `data`: the fix was a data change (a script or records edited).
    `configuration`: settings changed, no code. `no_change`: nothing changed (answered,
    duplicate, not reproducible, declined).
- **Never write `memory://...`, `N/A` or other text into `pr_uri`**: the write is refused
  ([Pull requests and resolution](#pull-requests-projectpullrequest-and-resolution)). A
  memory item closed without a pull request names its `memory://journal/<date>#<ID>` in a
  `Progress` note.
- **Close as no longer relevant** → `{"stage_id": <Canceled>}` (`<quiet>` +
  `tracking_disable`). Where the project lists that stage under "Stages Requiring a
  Resolution", the same write carries `"resolution": "no_change"`{% if ai_estimate %}; where it lists it
  under "Stages Requiring Actual Hours", `"actual_hours": <focused hours so far>`{% endif %}.
{% elif task_pr_uri %}
- **Close as done** → [revise the title and description](#revise-title-and-description-before-delivered)
  to what the work found, then `write {"pr_uri": "<PR URL, or memory://journal/<date>#<ID>>"}`,
  then `{"stage_id": <Delivered>}` — Delivered with empty `pr_uri` is
  blocked by a prod automation. Before closing a task that ran prod shell /
  bulk-data scripts, confirm each is attached (see Attachments).
{% else %}
- **Close as done** → [revise the title and description](#revise-title-and-description-before-delivered)
  to what the work found, then `write {"stage_id": <Delivered>}`, then a
  `Progress` note with the PR URL (or `memory://journal/<date>#<ID>`). Before
  closing a task that ran prod shell / bulk-data scripts, confirm each is
  attached (see Attachments).
{% endif %}
{% if not task_ai_driven %}
- **Close as no longer relevant** → `{"stage_id": <Canceled>}`{% if task_pr_uri %} (no pr_uri){% endif %}.
{% endif %}
- Stage rights: assignors/PM/assignees only — your own account needs one of
  those roles on the task's project to move it.

## Memory mirror linkage

State items carry `[odoo:<task_id>]` — see the behavior protocol
("State ↔ Odoo tickets") for the WHEN. This skill is the HOW (fields, flags, calls).
